+Creates a single, complex classification mapping by merging multiple simpler transfer functions using weighted boolean-like blending operations. This method allows users to intuitively explore features enhanced by different individual mappings and generate complex visualizations by aggregating simpler ones. Unlike single transfer function approaches, ensembles provide interactive swapping of blending strategies to rapidly explore volume datasets. Choose this for complex datasets where different features require distinct visualization strategies that must be combined.
 
 ### [3D Transfer Function](5777-3d-transfer-function.md)
-A transfer function that uses three dimensions to map volume data to visual properties: scalar data value, gradient magnitude, and second directional derivative along the gradient direction. It solves the problem of disambiguating complex boundary configurations where material boundaries overlap in data value and gradient magnitude but differ in second derivative. Choose this for highly complex datasets with ambiguous boundaries.
+Maps volume data to visual properties using three input dimensions: scalar data value, gradient magnitude, and the second directional derivative along the gradient direction. It solves the problem of disambiguating complex boundary configurations where different materials overlap in both scalar value and gradient magnitude but differ in their curvature at the interface. Unlike 2D transfer functions, which fail when boundaries intersect in the value-gradient plane, the 3D TF uses the second derivative to separate materials with similar values and gradient magnitudes but different boundary curvature. Choose this for highly complex datasets with ambiguous boundaries.
 
 ### [Size-Based Transfer Functions](5845-size-based-transfer-functions.md)
-A volume exploration technique that maps the relative size of local features in a volume to color and opacity, allowing classification of features based on size rather than scalar value alone. Choose this when feature size is a more discriminative property than intensity.
+Maps the local scale (relative size) of volumetric features to color and opacity, allowing the classification and segmentation of data based on spatial extent rather than scalar intensity alone. Unlike standard 1D transfer functions that treat all voxels with the same scalar value identically, SBTF distinguishes features with similar or identical scalar values by their size. It sets itself apart from pyramid-based multi-scale approaches by using continuous scale-space analysis to derive high-quality, continuous scale maps for a single dimension (size) orthogonal to scalar value. Choose this when feature size is a more discriminative property than intensity.
 
 ### [Probabilistic and Statistical Modeling](5932-probabilistic-and-statistical-modeling.md)
-Uses probabilistic models to determine transfer function parameters based on statistical properties of the data. Choose this for data-driven, objective classification methods.
